.TH GDD 1.SH NAMEgdd \- copy and convert blocks of a file.SH SYNOPSIS\fBgdd\fR[if=file] [of=file] [ibs=n] [obs=n] [bs=n] [cbs=n] [skip=n] [seek=n][count=n] [conv=conversion[,conversion...]].SH DESCRIPTION\fBgdd\fRis GNU dd, from the fileutils, under a name of its own.  It copies itsinput to its output in blocks, and can skip, count and convert on theway.  When it finishes it reports on the error output how many whole andpartial blocks went in and came out, as\fB0+1 records in\fR.\fR.SH OPERANDS.TP 12\fBif=\fIfile\fRread\fIfile\fRinstead of the standard input..TP 12\fBof=\fIfile\fRwrite\fIfile\fRinstead of the standard output.  On this disk\fBof=\fRcan only name a file that already exists; to make a new one, send thestandard output there with\fB>\fR.\fR.TP 12\fBbs=\fIn\fP, ibs=\fIn\fP, obs=\fIn\fP\fRthe block size for both sides, or for input and output separately..TP 12\fBskip=\fIn\fRskip\fIn\fRinput blocks before copying..TP 12\fBseek=\fIn\fRskip\fIn\fRoutput blocks before writing..TP 12\fBcount=\fIn\fRcopy only\fIn\fRinput blocks..TP 12\fBconv=\fIlist\fRconvert:\fBlcase\fRand\fBucase\fRchange case,\fBswab\fRswaps each pair of bytes,\fBascii\fRand\fBebcdic\fR(or\fBibm\fR)\fRtranslate the character set,\fBblock\fRand\fBunblock\fRpad or trim fixed\fBcbs=\fR-byte\fRrecords,\fBsync\fRpads every input block to size, and\fBnoerror\fRcarries on after a read error..PPA number may end in\fBb\fR(times 512),\fBk\fR(times 1024) or\fBw\fR(times 2), and two numbers may be multiplied with\fBx\fR,\fRas in\fBbs=2x80\fR.\fR.SH EXAMPLES.nfgdd if=notes bs=8 skip=1 count=1    bytes 8 to 15gdd if=notes conv=ucase > NOTES     the whole file in capitalsgdd if=disk.img bs=256 count=1 > s0 the first sector of an image.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRYour own OS-9 may have a utility called\fBdd\fR;\fRthis one is\fBgdd\fRso that both can be kept..SH SEE ALSOdump, split